  level: "Easy",
  q: "A 28-year-old primigravida at 34 weeks gestation presents for a routine check-up. Her blood pressure is 150/96 mmHg. A repeat measurement 4 hours later is 152/98 mmHg. Urine dipstick shows 2+ proteinuria. She has no headache, visual changes, or right upper quadrant pain. Laboratory results show normal platelet count, liver enzymes, and serum creatinine. What is the most accurate diagnosis?",
  opts: [
    "Gestational hypertension",
    "Preeclampsia without severe features",
    "Preeclampsia with severe features",
    "Chronic hypertension"
  ],
  ans: 1,
  exp: "Preeclampsia without severe features is defined by new-onset hypertension (≥140/90 mmHg on two occasions 4 hours apart) after 20 weeks of gestation, accompanied by proteinuria (≥300 mg/24h or dipstick ≥1+). Since her BP is <160/110 mmHg and she has no end-organ dysfunction (normal labs, no severe symptoms), it does not have severe features.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Gestational hypertension):</strong> Gestational hypertension is new-onset elevated blood pressure after 20 weeks *without* proteinuria or end-organ dysfunction.<br>• <strong>Option C (Preeclampsia with severe features):</strong> This would require BP ≥160/110 mmHg, thrombocytopenia (<100,000), impaired liver function, renal insufficiency, pulmonary edema, or new-onset visual/cerebral disturbances.<br>• <strong>Option D (Chronic hypertension):</strong> Chronic hypertension is defined as hypertension present before pregnancy or diagnosed before 20 weeks of gestation."
