  exp: "Normal pregnancy is characterized by a state of chronic, progesterone-driven hyperventilation, resulting in a physiological respiratory alkalosis with a baseline PaCO2 of ~28-32 mmHg. During a severe asthma attack, a patient should be hyperventilating even more to compensate for hypoxia. Therefore, a PaCO2 of 40 mmHg (which is 'normal' for a non-pregnant adult) in a pregnant asthmatic indicates respiratory muscle fatigue, failure to compensate, and impending respiratory arrest (pseudonormalization of PaCO2).",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (pH 7.44, PaCO2 28 mmHg):</strong> This reflects the normal physiological compensated respiratory alkalosis of pregnancy and an appropriate response to mild distress.<br>• <strong>Option B (pH 7.42, PaCO2 32 mmHg):</strong> This is also a typical, reassuring ABG in normal pregnancy.<br>• <strong>Option D (pH 7.46, PaCO2 26 mmHg):</strong> This indicates active hyperventilation, which is the expected initial response to an asthma attack before exhaustion sets in."
