  q: "A 21-year-old college student presents to the clinic on Monday morning. She had unprotected sexual intercourse on Friday night (approximately 60 hours ago). She does not want to get pregnant. She has a BMI of 22 kg/m2 and regular menstrual cycles. Which of the following is the most effective emergency contraception method available to her at this time?",
  opts: [
    "Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg oral pill (Plan B)",
    "Ulipristal acetate 30 mg oral pill",
    "Yuzpe regimen (high-dose combination OCPs)",
    "Copper T 380A Intrauterine Device insertion"
  ],
  ans: 3,
  exp: "The Copper T 380A IUD is the most effective method of emergency contraception overall (>99% effective) and can be inserted up to 5 days (120 hours) after unprotected intercourse. Once placed, it also provides excellent long-term reversible contraception for up to 10 years.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg pill):</strong> Plan B is effective if taken within 72 hours, but its efficacy sharply declines over time and it is significantly less effective than the Copper IUD.<br>• <strong>Option B (Ulipristal acetate):</strong> Ulipristal is a selective progesterone receptor modulator that is the most effective oral emergency contraceptive (effective up to 120 hours), but it is still less effective than the Copper IUD.<br>• <strong>Option C (Yuzpe regimen):</strong> The Yuzpe regimen (taking multiple standard combined OCPs) has the lowest efficacy and the highest side effect profile (nausea, vomiting) among emergency contraceptives."
