  q: "A 45-year-old G3P3 woman complains of involuntary urine leakage whenever she coughs, sneezes, or laughs out loud. She denies any pain with urination, frequent urges to void, or leaking at rest. She had three unassisted vaginal deliveries of macrosomic infants. A cough stress test in the lithotomy position shows immediate spurting of urine from the urethra. Urinalysis is normal. What is the fundamental pathophysiology underlying her condition?",
  opts: [
    "Detrusor muscle overactivity and uninhibited bladder contractions",
    "Urethral hypermobility due to loss of pelvic floor support",
    "Spinal cord lesion disrupting the sacral micturition center",
    "Fistulous tract formation between the bladder and the vagina"
  ],
  ans: 1,
  exp: "The patient has Stress Urinary Incontinence (SUI), defined as the involuntary leakage of urine upon exertion, sneezing, or coughing, due to increased intra-abdominal pressure. The pathophysiology is primarily related to weakness of the pelvic floor musculature and connective tissue (often from vaginal trauma/childbirth), leading to urethral hypermobility. Because the proximal urethra drops below the pelvic floor, increases in intra-abdominal pressure are not transmitted equally to the bladder and urethra, leading to leakage. Intrinsic sphincter deficiency (ISD) is the other mechanism of SUI.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Detrusor overactivity):</strong> This is the mechanism for Urge Incontinence (Overactive Bladder), presenting with a sudden, overwhelming urge to void followed by a large volume leak.<br>• <strong>Option C (Spinal cord lesion):</strong> This leads to neurogenic bladder, which typically presents as overflow incontinence (urinary retention and dribbling) or reflex incontinence, depending on the level of the lesion.<br>• <strong>Option D (Fistulous tract):</strong> A vesicovaginal fistula causes continuous, painless urine leakage directly from the vagina, regardless of physical activity or intra-abdominal pressure."
