  q: "A 65-year-old grand multiparous woman complains of a 'bulge' falling out of her vagina. During a POP-Q (Pelvic Organ Prolapse Quantification) examination while the patient performs a Valsalva maneuver, the leading edge of the prolapse is located at +3 cm relative to the hymen. The total vaginal length is measured at 8 cm. What stage of pelvic organ prolapse does this patient have?",
  opts: [
    "Stage I",
    "Stage II",
    "Stage III",
    "Stage IV"
  ],
  ans: 2,
  exp: "The Pelvic Organ Prolapse Quantification (POP-Q) system stages prolapse based on the leading edge relative to the hymen (0 cm). Stage I: leading edge is <-1 cm (above hymen). Stage II: leading edge is between -1 cm and +1 cm. Stage III: leading edge is >+1 cm but less than [Total Vaginal Length (TVL) - 2 cm]. Stage IV: complete eversion, leading edge is ≥ [TVL - 2 cm]. Here, the leading edge is at +3 cm. Since +3 cm is greater than +1 cm, and less than +6 cm (TVL of 8 cm - 2 cm = 6 cm), the prolapse is Stage III.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Stage I):</strong> The prolapse would be entirely internal, remaining higher than 1 cm above the hymenal ring (<-1).<br>• <strong>Option B (Stage II):</strong> The prolapse would reach the introitus, with the leading part measuring between 1 cm above (-1) and 1 cm below (+1) the hymen.<br>• <strong>Option D (Stage IV):</strong> This requires complete procidentia, where the entire vagina is everted. The leading edge must be at least +6 cm (TVL - 2cm) in this specific patient."
